[Music]

9
00:01:29,735 --> 00:01:31,755
[Music]

10
00:01:40,600 --> 00:01:53,759
It is now past midnight, Milky Way Standard Time. How is everyone doing? I'm Tsukino Mito. Tonight, from our special studio in orbit around Earth, we're broadcasting directly into the minds of everyone across the galaxy.

11
00:01:53,759 --> 00:02:12,000
[Jingle]

12
00:02:12,000 --> 00:02:13,740
And we're underway!

13
00:02:13,740 --> 00:02:17,920
This is the August 2026 edition of Mito Radio. Thank you for joining me.

14
00:02:17,920 --> 00:02:24,260
I got a little confused by some of the settings, but let's reset and get going.

15
00:02:24,260 --> 00:02:32,280
So then, as for what's been going on with me lately, well, I made my voice-acting debut.

16
00:02:32,280 --> 00:02:34,100
Did you all know?

17
00:02:34,100 --> 00:02:48,080
In a new take on Osamu Tezuka's classic Princess Knight, titled Ribbon Hero—THE RIBBON HERO—on Netflix, it's, well, how should I put it... not exactly a remake,

18
00:02:48,080 --> 00:02:51,680
but more like... an adaptation based on the original concept.

19
00:02:51,680 --> 00:02:58,060
It was newly adapted into an anime, and I voiced a character who appears in the original, Hecate.

20
00:02:58,060 --> 00:03:03,800
That's right—I actually got to make a small appearance as her voice actor.

21
00:03:03,800 --> 00:03:06,600
Has anyone seen it already?

22
00:03:06,600 --> 00:03:16,980
When I first received the offer, I honestly thought, “Whoa, finally!”

23
00:03:16,980 --> 00:03:23,520
You do occasionally see VTubers taking voice-acting roles, right?

24
00:03:23,520 --> 00:03:28,860
But until now, aside from playing Tsukino Mito, I'd never once received an offer.

25
00:03:28,860 --> 00:03:31,180
It wasn't that I was turning them down or anything.

26
00:03:31,180 --> 00:03:34,840
I genuinely had never received one, so I thought, “Finally!”

27
00:03:34,840 --> 00:03:45,800
And Princess Knight is such a huge name that the whole offer felt almost unbelievable, but I worked up the courage and accepted it.

28
00:03:45,800 --> 00:03:50,580
In Virtual-san wa Miteiru, I played Tsukino Mito—because I am Tsukino Mito.

29
00:03:50,580 --> 00:03:57,660
But I'd never voiced a completely different person before.

30
00:03:57,660 --> 00:04:01,540
So I was extremely nervous.

31
00:04:01,540 --> 00:04:04,420
It had been a while since I'd felt that nervous.

32
00:04:04,420 --> 00:04:09,660
It felt like the first time since my solo concert.

33
00:04:09,660 --> 00:04:14,620
And, well, I recorded my part alone.

34
00:04:14,620 --> 00:04:16,360
None of the other voice actors were there.

35
00:04:16,360 --> 00:04:24,380
I suppose that was because recording together with everyone else would have required reserving a lot more time in everyone's schedules.

36
00:04:24,380 --> 00:04:31,260
I'm not sure whether that decision came from ANYCOLOR or the production side, but I think they were being considerate by arranging it as a one-day session.

37
00:04:31,260 --> 00:04:33,260
So it was recorded separately.

38
00:04:33,260 --> 00:04:35,920
That's how it went.

39
00:04:35,920 --> 00:04:43,260
So I haven't met any of my co-stars at all, but still, it was amazing.

40
00:04:43,260 --> 00:04:51,420
Maybe because it was my first voice-acting role, and partly to help ease my nerves, they praised me a lot.

41
00:04:51,420 --> 00:04:57,480
There on set, I really regained some of my self-confidence.

42
00:04:57,480 --> 00:05:01,260
Though the stream earlier failed and disappeared once, so there was that.

43
00:05:01,260 --> 00:05:05,260
It vanished once, but yes, my self-esteem got quite a boost.

44
00:05:05,260 --> 00:05:17,260
I really was incredibly nervous, so I spoke with my voice-actor friend Ayaneru and asked her for advice.

45
00:05:17,260 --> 00:05:21,260
I tried all sorts of things through trial and error.

46
00:05:21,260 --> 00:05:25,260
More than anything, I hope you'll watch Princess Knight.

47
00:05:25,260 --> 00:05:28,260
Please check it out on Netflix.

48
00:05:28,260 --> 00:05:29,260
I hope you enjoy it!

49
00:05:29,260 --> 00:05:31,260
Please support it.

50
00:05:31,260 --> 00:05:32,260
Yes.

51
00:05:32,260 --> 00:05:38,260
Ayaneru gave me all kinds of thoughtful advice, and I was truly grateful to her.

52
00:05:38,260 --> 00:05:42,260
So, with that said... shall we begin?

53
00:05:42,260 --> 00:05:54,260
This time, now that I've joined the ranks of female voice actors, I thought I'd like to talk with someone who has a cute voice.

54
00:05:54,260 --> 00:05:59,260
On Mito Radio, the person we connect to is fairly random.

55
00:05:59,260 --> 00:06:01,260
Who will it be?

56
00:06:01,260 --> 00:06:06,260
Mito Radio begins at my frequency of 3.1 opeta-hertz.

57
00:06:06,260 --> 00:06:08,260
Please take a look here.

58
00:06:08,260 --> 00:06:11,260
Yes, the dial is currently set to 310.

59
00:06:11,260 --> 00:06:16,260
This radio show tunes from there to the guest's frequency.

60
00:06:16,260 --> 00:06:21,260
The frequency we connect to is random each time, so I'm excited to see what happens.

61
00:06:21,260 --> 00:06:24,260
Let's turn the dial.

62
00:06:24,260 --> 00:06:28,260
Oh! Where will it stop? Where will it stop?

63
00:06:29,260 --> 00:06:44,260
Nijisanji? Are we finally connecting to the whole agency?

64
00:06:44,260 --> 00:06:48,260
[Jingle]

65
00:06:48,260 --> 00:06:51,260
[Jingle]

66
00:06:51,260 --> 00:06:53,260
[Jingle]

67
00:06:53,260 --> 00:06:55,260
[Jingle]

68
00:06:55,260 --> 00:06:57,260
[Jingle]

69
00:06:57,260 --> 00:06:59,620
Phew...

70
00:06:59,620 --> 00:07:02,040
We've finally made it.

71
00:07:02,040 --> 00:07:03,380
The Demon King's castle.

72
00:07:03,380 --> 00:07:06,300
It really took a long time to get here.

73
00:07:06,300 --> 00:07:08,860
It was a long and grueling battle.

74
00:07:08,860 --> 00:07:12,620
Everyone in the hero's party except me was wiped out.

75
00:07:12,620 --> 00:07:15,560
But don't worry—I brought their coffins with me.

76
00:07:15,560 --> 00:07:19,480
Look, this is the Demon King's castle.

77
00:07:19,480 --> 00:07:22,520
I wanted to bring everyone all the way here too.

78
00:07:22,520 --> 00:07:24,360
Right, everyone?

79
00:07:24,360 --> 00:07:28,020
I can practically see what everyone wants to say.

80
00:07:28,020 --> 00:07:30,760
It's as though our hearts are connected.

81
00:07:30,760 --> 00:07:36,260
I know exactly what everyone is thinking.

82
00:07:36,260 --> 00:07:39,160
But don't worry.

83
00:07:39,160 --> 00:07:44,600
I'm sure I can reach the Demon King even on my own.

84
00:07:44,600 --> 00:07:56,000
Well, everyone in my party was weak, so unbelievably weak that all I could bring were their coffins, but onward we go—to defeat the Demon King!

85
00:07:56,000 --> 00:08:13,360
At last, we've made it.

86
00:08:13,360 --> 00:08:17,700
We're only one step away from defeating the Demon King.

87
00:08:17,700 --> 00:08:21,880
Now, show yourself, Demon King!

88
00:08:24,360 --> 00:08:25,360
Huh?

89
00:08:25,360 --> 00:08:27,680
Huh? Huh? Huh?

90
00:08:27,680 --> 00:08:30,320
Wait—isn't that you, Class Rep?

91
00:08:30,320 --> 00:08:32,720
What are you doing in a place like this?

92
00:08:32,720 --> 00:08:34,800
It's a Liver with a cute voice!

93
00:08:34,800 --> 00:08:37,360
What? Am I hallucinating?

94
00:08:37,360 --> 00:08:39,860
Why are you here? I could ask you the same thing, Lulu-chan!

95
00:08:39,860 --> 00:08:41,400
No, that's my question!

96
00:08:41,400 --> 00:08:48,060
I was just out for an ordinary walk, then I got lost and ended up here.

97
00:08:48,060 --> 00:08:48,940
All the way out here?

98
00:08:48,940 --> 00:08:52,360
Though I suppose you'd be fine making it here alone, Lulu-chan.

99
00:08:52,360 --> 00:08:53,920
This is part of my walking route, you see.

100
00:08:54,360 --> 00:08:55,360
I was just taking a walk...

101
00:08:55,360 --> 00:08:57,860
Are you too powerful or what?

102
00:08:57,860 --> 00:09:01,120
Hey, you didn't happen to see anyone around here, did you?

103
00:09:01,120 --> 00:09:02,920
Someone resembling a Demon King?

104
00:09:02,920 --> 00:09:06,600
No, there's nobody here besides me.

105
00:09:06,600 --> 00:09:07,360
Huh?

106
00:09:07,360 --> 00:09:12,900
It looks like nobody else is here, so why don't we have a little chat while we're here?

107
00:09:12,900 --> 00:09:13,740
Sure.

108
00:09:13,740 --> 00:09:16,600
Maybe another hero already defeated him.

109
00:09:16,600 --> 00:09:19,920
For now, shall we have a chat instead?

110
00:09:19,920 --> 00:09:21,020
Okay!

111
00:09:21,020 --> 00:09:23,360
More importantly, welcome back!

112
00:09:23,360 --> 00:09:24,160
Yes! More importantly, welcome back!

113
00:09:24,360 --> 00:09:26,120
Yes! Well, for the moment...

114
00:09:26,120 --> 00:09:27,360
Though this is the second time you've welcomed me back.

115
00:09:27,360 --> 00:09:29,440
I'm back! I'm back.

116
00:09:29,440 --> 00:09:31,380
Huh? Huh?

117
00:09:31,380 --> 00:09:33,420
Still, I'm really happy.

118
00:09:33,420 --> 00:09:36,980
We did get to talk once before—during a call-in stream, though.

119
00:09:36,980 --> 00:09:37,720
Right, right, right.

120
00:09:37,720 --> 00:09:44,640
We had that opportunity, but this feels incredibly moving.

121
00:09:44,640 --> 00:09:45,920
It really does.

122
00:09:45,920 --> 00:09:50,480
I mean, your return genuinely shocked me.

123
00:09:50,480 --> 00:09:51,360
Your comeback...

124
00:09:51,360 --> 00:09:53,000
Yes, of course.

125
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:53,360
I figured it probably would.

126
00:09:53,360 --> 00:09:55,360
I figured as much.

127
00:09:55,360 --> 00:09:56,860
Still, you know...

128
00:09:56,860 --> 00:10:00,600
I agonized over what to do as I prepared to return.

129
00:10:00,600 --> 00:10:01,100
Mm-hmm.

130
00:10:01,100 --> 00:10:08,480
I wondered whether I should contact people beforehand and say that I might be coming back.

131
00:10:08,480 --> 00:10:10,280
Right, right, right.

132
00:10:10,280 --> 00:10:17,740
But I wasn't sure, so I was feeling out how people might react.

133
00:10:17,740 --> 00:10:20,640
As for me, I wondered whether it would be better to wait until after I returned.

134
00:10:20,640 --> 00:10:22,360
But then I messaged you on LINE at lightning speed!

135
00:10:22,360 --> 00:10:23,200
At lightning speed!

136
00:10:23,360 --> 00:10:24,180
I really did message you immediately.

137
00:10:24,180 --> 00:10:24,800
As soon as I heard.

138
00:10:24,800 --> 00:10:28,240
I was so happy.

139
00:10:28,240 --> 00:10:31,720
I was planning to message you first, so I never expected...

140
00:10:31,720 --> 00:10:32,420
Right.

141
00:10:32,420 --> 00:10:34,160
I never expected the Class Rep to contact me first.

142
00:10:34,160 --> 00:10:38,120
We had quietly exchanged LINE contacts in the past, you see.

143
00:10:38,120 --> 00:10:40,120
So I immediately sent her a LINE message.

144
00:10:40,120 --> 00:10:47,240
We've actually gone to real-life escape rooms together, right, right, and hung out in private.

145
00:10:47,240 --> 00:10:53,160
Exactly. So I'd like to read our first question, which touches on that too.

146
00:10:53,160 --> 00:10:54,680
Okay.

147
00:10:54,680 --> 00:10:57,380
Here it is.

148
00:10:57,380 --> 00:11:00,440
“Good evening, Class Rep and Lulu-chan.

149
00:11:00,440 --> 00:11:05,460
“Congratulations on Lulu-chan's return after four and a half years, and on this Mito Radio appearance.

150
00:11:05,460 --> 00:11:11,520
“I was truly surprised when I saw the announcement, and I'm delighted that we can hear from you again.

151
00:11:11,520 --> 00:11:19,780
“As the possibility of returning gradually began to feel real, “how did you initially feel, Lulu-chan?

152
00:11:19,780 --> 00:11:25,120
“If you can share, I'd love to hear when you began considering a return and any behind-the-scenes stories.

153
00:11:25,120 --> 00:11:30,220
“And Class Rep, how did you react when you learned Lulu-chan was returning?

154
00:11:30,220 --> 00:11:36,340
“I'd love to hear your honest first reaction and anything that happened at the time, “as much as you're able to share.

155
00:11:36,340 --> 00:11:38,100
“I'm looking forward to hearing you both talk.”

156
00:11:38,100 --> 00:11:39,540
From Mito Radio listener Kon-chan.

157
00:11:39,540 --> 00:11:41,520
Thank you.

158
00:11:41,520 --> 00:11:42,880
Thank you.

159
00:11:42,880 --> 00:11:44,800
Thank you.

160
00:11:44,800 --> 00:11:49,240
Come to think of it, I don't know much about that either, so I'm curious.

161
00:11:49,240 --> 00:11:51,700
Only share whatever you're comfortable discussing, of course.

162
00:11:51,700 --> 00:11:58,660
Right. I hadn't told many other Livers about it either.

163
00:11:58,660 --> 00:11:59,160
I thought so.

164
00:11:59,160 --> 00:12:05,700
I think only a very small number of people knew before my return.

165
00:12:05,700 --> 00:12:17,880
Honestly, in terms of when it began feeling real, I was incredibly nervous.

166
00:12:17,880 --> 00:12:18,700
Really?

167
00:12:18,700 --> 00:12:19,200
Honestly.

168
00:12:19,240 --> 00:12:20,240
Yes.

169
00:12:20,240 --> 00:12:37,600
From before the comeback stream until after it, everything felt hazy and unreal.

170
00:12:37,600 --> 00:12:44,980
It was only after the comeback stream, when I started playing games and streaming again, that I finally thought, “Oh, I really am back. I made it back here.”

171
00:12:44,980 --> 00:12:46,960
Wow.

172
00:12:46,960 --> 00:12:48,320
That's how it was.

173
00:12:48,320 --> 00:12:54,680
I received this question quite a lot too: I received this one quite often—people asking, people asking me, “Which made you more nervous, your debut stream or your comeback stream?”

174
00:12:54,680 --> 00:13:00,160
Honestly, they may have made me equally nervous.

175
00:13:00,160 --> 00:13:00,580
Wow.

176
00:13:00,580 --> 00:13:07,420
It felt like doing my very first stream all over again.

177
00:13:07,420 --> 00:13:09,640
And in a way, that's exactly what it was.

178
00:13:09,640 --> 00:13:17,680
So much time had passed since I graduated from Nijisanji.

179
00:13:17,680 --> 00:13:19,220
That would naturally make you anxious.

180
00:13:19,240 --> 00:13:33,940
I knew there would be people who didn't know me, so I thought carefully about how to introduce and explain myself.

181
00:13:33,940 --> 00:13:42,240
That makes sense. The audience's age and gender makeup seems to have changed quite a bit too.

182
00:13:42,240 --> 00:13:43,420
Compared with when Lulu-chan was here.

183
00:13:43,420 --> 00:13:46,820
Yes, I could really feel how much it had changed.

184
00:13:46,820 --> 00:13:47,760
Right.

185
00:13:47,760 --> 00:13:48,600
Mm-hmm.

186
00:13:48,600 --> 00:13:49,220
It felt quite different.

187
00:13:49,240 --> 00:13:55,180
I noticed that many new viewers had joined.

188
00:13:55,180 --> 00:13:56,860
There were audiences I hadn't encountered much before.

189
00:13:56,860 --> 00:13:58,660
Absolutely.

190
00:13:58,660 --> 00:13:59,820
Really? I see.

191
00:13:59,820 --> 00:14:10,940
Besides the age range, the biggest change I noticed was how many more female viewers there are now.

192
00:14:10,940 --> 00:14:19,160
Right. Across Nijisanji as a whole, yes. the gender split is now almost fifty-fifty, which would have been unimaginable before.

193
00:14:19,240 --> 00:14:20,520
The ratio really has changed.

194
00:14:20,520 --> 00:14:32,360
Before I graduated, it varied by Liver, but I remember there being many more male viewers.

195
00:14:32,360 --> 00:14:37,120
After returning, I especially noticed it when I attended NijiFes.

196
00:14:37,120 --> 00:14:41,760
There were so many more women, and that made me really happy.

197
00:14:41,760 --> 00:14:47,340
Right. It must make everything feel even more different from before.

198
00:14:47,340 --> 00:14:49,220
That was part of what made me a little anxious.

199
00:14:49,240 --> 00:14:52,920
I could tell that the overall atmosphere had changed.

200
00:14:52,920 --> 00:14:58,080
But of course you'd be nervous.

201
00:14:58,080 --> 00:15:00,940
Yes, I was.

202
00:15:00,940 --> 00:15:02,660
Extremely nervous.

203
00:15:02,660 --> 00:15:07,380
Had you been considering returning for quite a while?

204
00:15:07,380 --> 00:15:10,420
Yes, for a fairly long time.

205
00:15:10,420 --> 00:15:18,820
To be specific, it goes back to last year's NijiFes.

206
00:15:19,240 --> 00:15:20,040
Last year?

207
00:15:20,040 --> 00:15:22,380
NijiFes?

208
00:15:22,380 --> 00:15:29,280
Yes, yes.

209
00:15:29,280 --> 00:15:31,200
We had already begun discussing it around the time before last year's event.

210
00:15:31,200 --> 00:15:41,580
We even discussed whether I might quietly leave a signature or message at NijiFes.

211
00:15:41,580 --> 00:15:49,080
Really? So it wasn't merely that you wanted to return—it was already almost decided?

212
00:15:49,240 --> 00:16:05,000
Not quite. They gave me opportunities to discuss it, and we considered various possibilities. The conversations began a long time ago, but the process was extended.

213
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:05,880
Oh, I see.

214
00:16:05,880 --> 00:16:10,480
So it had been in the works for quite a while.

215
00:16:10,480 --> 00:16:11,280
Yes.

216
00:16:11,280 --> 00:16:12,740
Interesting.

217
00:16:12,740 --> 00:16:18,020
It may have been much longer ago than people imagine.

218
00:16:18,020 --> 00:16:19,040
Mm-hmm. That's fascinating.

219
00:16:19,040 --> 00:16:24,540
If that NijiFes idea had happened, it would have caused an enormous commotion.

220
00:16:24,540 --> 00:16:27,540
It really would have.

221
00:16:27,540 --> 00:16:34,040
Everyone would have completely freaked out. I certainly would have.

222
00:16:34,040 --> 00:16:38,720
Still, I think the way it ultimately happened was better.

223
00:16:38,720 --> 00:16:50,720
I'm glad. I was genuinely worried about what the other Livers would think.

224
00:16:50,940 --> 00:16:59,860
But once I actually opened the lid, so to speak, everyone was genuinely kind. They contacted me privately to say they were happy I was back,

225
00:16:59,860 --> 00:17:03,520
and asked to collaborate since we hadn't been able to before. That made me so happy.

226
00:17:03,520 --> 00:17:08,560
You contacted me too, Class Rep.

227
00:17:08,560 --> 00:17:13,360
That finally made me feel a little reassured.

228
00:17:13,360 --> 00:17:15,640
I'm glad. When you retired, I was genuinely devastated.

229
00:17:15,640 --> 00:17:15,820
Seriously.

230
00:17:15,820 --> 00:17:21,100
I was so sad. I think I told you that too.

231
00:17:21,100 --> 00:17:25,300
You did. You really did.

232
00:17:25,300 --> 00:17:31,180
It almost felt like Nijisanji itself was over.

233
00:17:31,180 --> 00:17:36,920
I was feeling pretty low then too.

234
00:17:36,920 --> 00:17:46,960
Around the same time, other people left as well, so I remember thinking, “Oh, it's all over.”

235
00:17:46,960 --> 00:17:59,600
It wasn't exactly a wave of graduations, but several people did graduate around the same time. Still, we held out until your return!

236
00:17:59,600 --> 00:18:07,960
We really were waiting. Nijisanji was waiting for Suzuhara. That genuinely makes me happy.

237
00:18:07,960 --> 00:18:10,600
I never imagined that I'd be able to do this again.

238
00:18:10,600 --> 00:18:16,960
I'm so happy that we can talk and collaborate like this, because we couldn't do any of that before.

239
00:18:16,960 --> 00:18:22,120
It still doesn't quite feel real.

240
00:18:22,120 --> 00:18:28,240
I even wondered whether there was a chance you'd return looking completely different.

241
00:18:28,240 --> 00:18:28,940
What?

242
00:18:28,940 --> 00:18:30,200
Like...

243
00:18:30,200 --> 00:18:31,480
What do you mean?

244
00:18:31,480 --> 00:18:33,540
Like a grown-up version of yourself.

245
00:18:33,540 --> 00:18:38,300
I thought you might return in a completely new, version-two form.

246
00:18:38,300 --> 00:18:39,800
That possibility crossed my mind.

247
00:18:39,800 --> 00:18:48,820
But you came back exactly as you were, and it really felt as though time, which had stopped, suddenly began moving again.

248
00:18:48,820 --> 00:18:50,920
Like waking from cryogenic sleep.

249
00:18:50,920 --> 00:18:53,180
Exactly. Nothing has changed.

250
00:18:53,180 --> 00:18:58,420
You came back just as you were, and that genuinely surprised me.

251
00:18:58,420 --> 00:19:01,860
Even your streaming style is almost unchanged. It's incredible.

252
00:19:01,860 --> 00:19:09,560
That's true. It really feels as though time stopped back then and has only just started moving again.

253
00:19:09,800 --> 00:19:10,920
Yeah.

254
00:19:10,920 --> 00:19:15,100
Though I do feel a little left behind because so many things have evolved.

255
00:19:15,100 --> 00:19:17,660
Oh, so you still feel that way?

256
00:19:17,660 --> 00:19:19,540
Yes, but I'm only getting started.

257
00:19:19,540 --> 00:19:21,820
You'll get used to it.

258
00:19:21,820 --> 00:19:23,940
I'm taking it slowly, but I do want to adjust.

259
00:19:23,940 --> 00:19:27,140
Surprisingly, not that much has changed.

260
00:19:27,140 --> 00:19:28,760
It hasn't?

261
00:19:28,760 --> 00:19:31,340
No, surprisingly little has changed.

262
00:19:31,340 --> 00:19:35,800
Plenty of people who watched before are still watching now.

263
00:19:35,800 --> 00:19:39,780
There are things that haven't changed, certainly.

264
00:19:39,800 --> 00:19:47,820
That continuity is nice, but after such a long gap I can still feel the differences.

265
00:19:47,820 --> 00:19:48,820
I see.

266
00:19:48,820 --> 00:19:49,820
Mm-hmm.

267
00:19:49,820 --> 00:19:54,820
But you'll be fine. You really haven't changed at all.

268
00:19:54,820 --> 00:20:01,160
I was thinking I should relearn OBS so I don't fall behind.

269
00:20:01,160 --> 00:20:05,800
I was seriously panicking!

270
00:20:05,800 --> 00:20:08,920
The technical side of things too, you know.

271
00:20:08,920 --> 00:20:09,640
Exactly.

272
00:20:09,800 --> 00:20:18,820
Before the stream, both of us were scrambling around because of our equipment.

273
00:20:18,820 --> 00:20:19,820
That's right.

274
00:20:19,820 --> 00:20:23,820
I really do feel left behind. I'm in the same position.

275
00:20:23,820 --> 00:20:24,820
We'll catch up from here.

276
00:20:24,820 --> 00:20:29,820
And Mito Radio itself—the previous episode with Fuwa-san was a year ago.

277
00:20:29,820 --> 00:20:33,820
So it really has been a long time.

278
00:20:33,820 --> 00:20:38,820
Right. Did I do it fairly often when Lulu-chan was still here?

279
00:20:38,820 --> 00:20:39,820
I'm not sure.

280
00:20:39,820 --> 00:20:40,820
Do you remember appearing on Mito Radio?

281
00:20:40,820 --> 00:20:45,820
Right. I'd like to properly revive the show.

282
00:20:45,820 --> 00:20:49,820
Is it produced rather spontaneously?

283
00:20:49,820 --> 00:20:57,820
Yes, very spontaneously—but the guests are decided well in advance.

284
00:20:57,820 --> 00:21:06,820
After the episode with Fuwa-san ended and Lulu-chan had been back for a little while, I kept thinking, “Lulu-chan, Lulu-chan.”

285
00:21:06,820 --> 00:21:07,820
That makes me so happy!

286
00:21:07,820 --> 00:21:08,820
Yes!

287
00:21:08,820 --> 00:21:09,820
Are you sure I deserve that?

288
00:21:09,820 --> 00:21:11,820
Of course.

289
00:21:11,820 --> 00:21:12,820
I'm incredibly happy.

290
00:21:12,820 --> 00:21:13,820
I'm the one wondering whether this is really okay!

291
00:21:13,820 --> 00:21:16,820
I wondered whether it was okay for me to be your guest after the show's year-long absence.

292
00:21:16,820 --> 00:21:21,820
No, no, absolutely! I'm the one asking whether it's okay.

293
00:21:21,820 --> 00:21:22,820
It's more than okay, honestly.

294
00:21:22,820 --> 00:21:23,820
I've wanted this for ages.

295
00:21:23,820 --> 00:21:30,820
At the beginning, you were talking about your voice-acting work, right, right.

296
00:21:30,820 --> 00:21:32,820
Do you get nervous, Class Rep?

297
00:21:32,820 --> 00:21:37,820
Of course I do! If anything, don't you get nervous, Lulu-chan?

298
00:21:37,820 --> 00:21:39,820
I just can't imagine you being nervous.

299
00:21:39,820 --> 00:21:47,820
Maybe we both thought that about each other. I was quite nervous even when I met you in person.

300
00:21:47,820 --> 00:21:48,820
Seriously?

301
00:21:48,820 --> 00:21:52,820
Yes, I was extremely nervous the first time we met.

302
00:21:52,820 --> 00:22:06,820
Really? These days I don't get nervous as often, mm-hmm, but Lulu-chan gives off this image of being incredibly tough, physically and mentally strong,

303
00:22:06,820 --> 00:22:09,820
so I assumed you simply didn't get nervous.

304
00:22:09,820 --> 00:22:16,820
No, I think I'm actually fairly prone to nerves.

305
00:22:16,820 --> 00:22:18,820
Really? That's unexpected.

306
00:22:18,820 --> 00:22:28,820
Maybe it doesn't show. People don't realize I'm nervous, but behind the scenes my heart is about to explode.

307
00:22:28,820 --> 00:22:29,820
Wow.

308
00:22:29,820 --> 00:22:30,820
That's how it is.

309
00:22:30,820 --> 00:22:31,820
So you have a poker face.

310
00:22:31,820 --> 00:22:32,820
It just doesn't show.

311
00:22:32,820 --> 00:22:34,820
Maybe so.

312
00:22:34,820 --> 00:22:35,820
Mm-hmm.

313
00:22:35,820 --> 00:22:37,820
I definitely get nervous too.

314
00:22:37,820 --> 00:22:39,820
Singing streams make me extremely nervous.

315
00:22:39,820 --> 00:22:42,820
That may be the most surprising thing you've said.

316
00:22:42,820 --> 00:22:47,820
Unlike a live performance, anyone can go back and watch a stream at any time.

317
00:22:47,820 --> 00:22:51,820
Anything that remains permanently —I understand. —makes me really nervous.

318
00:22:51,820 --> 00:22:52,820
I understand.

319
00:22:52,820 --> 00:22:54,820
Songs and other creative works especially...

320
00:22:54,820 --> 00:22:55,820
Right.

321
00:22:55,820 --> 00:23:01,820
When you think about them remaining available and being watched in the future, exactly, you get rather nervous.

322
00:23:01,820 --> 00:23:02,820
Absolutely.

323
00:23:02,820 --> 00:23:03,820
Mm-hmm.

324
00:23:03,820 --> 00:23:05,820
Even OBS today...

325
00:23:05,820 --> 00:23:10,820
Were you nervous during your debut stream too?

326
00:23:10,820 --> 00:23:13,820
Surely I must have been.

327
00:23:13,820 --> 00:23:14,820
Oh?

328
00:23:14,820 --> 00:23:15,820
Though I was streaming on Mirrativ back then.

329
00:23:15,820 --> 00:23:16,820
So you do get nervous.

330
00:23:16,820 --> 00:23:18,820
My Mirrativ stream disconnected eight times.

331
00:23:18,820 --> 00:23:20,820
Eight times? That's outrageous!

332
00:23:20,820 --> 00:23:23,820
This time it only failed once, so that's growth.

333
00:23:23,820 --> 00:23:30,820
I see—you improve every day. VTubers truly are growth-driven content.

334
00:23:30,820 --> 00:23:32,820
My connection was unbelievably weak.

335
00:23:32,820 --> 00:23:38,820
I suppose VTubers still weren't —mm-hmm—anywhere near as widespread as they are now.

336
00:23:38,820 --> 00:23:39,820
Right.

337
00:23:39,820 --> 00:23:47,820
Rather than already being a huge phenomenon, from my perspective, you and the other early members were the ones who built it into one.

338
00:23:47,820 --> 00:23:48,820
Seriously?

339
00:23:48,820 --> 00:23:53,820
Yes. You felt like legends, so of course I was nervous.

340
00:23:53,820 --> 00:23:54,820
Oh, come on!

341
00:23:54,820 --> 00:23:55,820
That's why.

342
00:23:55,820 --> 00:23:59,820
Relax your shoulders, Lulu!

343
00:23:59,820 --> 00:24:01,820
[Laughter]

344
00:24:02,820 --> 00:24:04,820
Would it be all right if I...

345
00:24:04,820 --> 00:24:05,820
Lulu!

346
00:24:05,820 --> 00:24:06,820
Like last time...

347
00:24:06,820 --> 00:24:07,820
Oh, speak casually?

348
00:24:07,820 --> 00:24:08,820
Yes, casually.

349
00:24:08,820 --> 00:24:13,820
You can speak casually to me for the rest of our lives!

350
00:24:13,820 --> 00:24:17,820
I honestly feel that way about every Nijisanji Liver.

351
00:24:17,820 --> 00:24:19,820
Casual speech is completely fine.

352
00:24:19,820 --> 00:24:21,820
That's how this Mito feels.

353
00:24:21,820 --> 00:24:22,820
Really?

354
00:24:22,820 --> 00:24:27,820
Wait—if someone combines this with the same BGM, the music should cancel out and isolate our voices.

355
00:24:27,820 --> 00:24:28,820
What?

356
00:24:28,820 --> 00:24:31,820
Could you please not resort to technology like that?

357
00:24:32,820 --> 00:24:34,820
Seriously...

358
00:24:34,820 --> 00:24:35,820
[Laughter]

359
00:24:35,820 --> 00:24:39,820
Still, I think speaking casually would be nice.

360
00:24:39,820 --> 00:24:40,820
Exactly.

361
00:24:40,820 --> 00:24:41,820
Huh?

362
00:24:41,820 --> 00:24:47,820
Hey, I'd better write down the name of this BGM.

363
00:24:47,820 --> 00:24:50,820
[Laughter]

364
00:24:50,820 --> 00:24:53,820
I'm genuinely so happy.

365
00:24:53,820 --> 00:24:54,820
That makes me happy.

366
00:24:54,820 --> 00:24:57,820
I'm nervous, but more than anything, I think the happiness is really winning out.

367
00:24:57,820 --> 00:24:58,820
Really?

368
00:24:58,820 --> 00:24:59,820
Huh?

369
00:24:59,820 --> 00:25:00,820
Truly.

370
00:25:00,820 --> 00:25:01,820
I'm happy.

371
00:25:01,820 --> 00:25:02,820
Extremely happy.

372
00:25:02,820 --> 00:25:04,820
Shall we move on to the next message?

373
00:25:04,820 --> 00:25:05,820
Yes.

374
00:25:05,820 --> 00:25:09,820
Let's see, which one should we choose?

375
00:25:09,820 --> 00:25:11,820
Oh, this one interests me.

376
00:25:11,820 --> 00:25:12,820
Yes?

377
00:25:12,820 --> 00:25:16,820
“Hello, Class Rep and Lulu-chan. I have a question for Lulu-chan.

378
00:25:16,820 --> 00:25:26,820
“During the Class Rep's reverse call-in stream, there was a topic about how you discovered Nijisanji and why you wanted to join, but time ran out before you could answer.

379
00:25:26,820 --> 00:25:31,820
“What first led you to discover Nijisanji and made you want to join?

380
00:25:31,820 --> 00:25:36,820
“Were there any Livers you watched then, or memorable events? I'd love to hear about them.

381
00:25:36,820 --> 00:25:41,820
“I'm really looking forward to hearing you both talk, and I'll continue supporting both of you.”

382
00:25:41,820 --> 00:25:44,820
This is from Mito Radio listener Yamada-san.

383
00:25:44,820 --> 00:25:46,820
Thank you.

384
00:25:46,820 --> 00:25:48,820
Thank you.

385
00:25:48,820 --> 00:25:50,820
Have you ever talked about this before?

386
00:25:50,820 --> 00:25:59,820
Why I joined Nijisanji? I may have mentioned it in a chatting stream shortly after my debut.

387
00:25:59,820 --> 00:26:00,820
Oh?

388
00:26:00,820 --> 00:26:02,820
I want to hear it.

389
00:26:02,820 --> 00:26:12,820
My first exposure to VTubers was through Kizuna AI.

390
00:26:12,820 --> 00:26:13,820
So that was quite a long time ago.

391
00:26:13,820 --> 00:26:20,820
Yes, Kizuna AI was genuinely the first one I watched, and that introduced me to VTubers.

392
00:26:20,820 --> 00:26:25,820
The first Nijisanji member I watched was actually Honma Himawari-chan.

393
00:26:25,820 --> 00:26:26,820
Oh, really?

394
00:26:26,820 --> 00:26:29,820
I saw Hima-chan and thought she was incredibly cute.

395
00:26:30,820 --> 00:26:31,820
I see.

396
00:26:31,820 --> 00:26:35,820
That's how I discovered Nijisanji, and the next person I watched was you, Class Rep.

397
00:26:35,820 --> 00:26:37,820
Oh, really?

398
00:26:37,820 --> 00:26:39,820
Yes, yes.

399
00:26:39,820 --> 00:26:41,820
Do you remember what kind of stream you watched from Hima-chan?

400
00:26:41,820 --> 00:26:43,820
I think it was a chatting stream.

401
00:26:43,820 --> 00:26:44,820
Oh.

402
00:26:44,820 --> 00:26:56,820
I watched her chatting and thought she was adorable. It made me realize this whole world existed; until then, I had never really watched livestreams.

403
00:26:56,820 --> 00:26:57,820
I see.

404
00:26:57,820 --> 00:26:58,820
That's what sparked my interest.

405
00:26:58,820 --> 00:26:59,820
Right.

406
00:26:59,820 --> 00:27:08,820
Then I watched you, Class Rep, and got a sense of Nijisanji's overall atmosphere.

407
00:27:08,820 --> 00:27:09,820
Right, right.

408
00:27:09,820 --> 00:27:10,820
How should I describe it...

409
00:27:10,820 --> 00:27:11,820
I see.

410
00:27:11,820 --> 00:27:25,820
It seemed like a group where everyone was free to do interesting things. I loved that, and it made me want to join Nijisanji.

411
00:27:25,820 --> 00:27:27,820
Oh, I see.

412
00:27:27,820 --> 00:27:28,820
Mm-hmm.

413
00:27:28,820 --> 00:27:39,820
So Hima-chan and Nijisanji are what made you want to become a VTuber in the first place.

414
00:27:39,820 --> 00:27:41,820
Yes, it began there.

415
00:27:41,820 --> 00:27:42,820
I see.

416
00:27:42,820 --> 00:27:49,820
That's how I discovered VTubers and livestreaming and became really interested.

417
00:27:49,820 --> 00:27:52,820
So you hadn't watched much game commentary before?

418
00:27:52,820 --> 00:27:57,820
I watched it back in the ancient days—on Nico Nico Douga and the like.

419
00:27:57,820 --> 00:27:59,820
Actually, so did I.

420
00:27:59,820 --> 00:28:05,820
Back then, I watched a lot of recorded game-commentary videos rather than live streams.

421
00:28:05,820 --> 00:28:13,820
Same here. I had never really watched anyone live, but I watched game-commentary videos on Nico Nico.

422
00:28:13,820 --> 00:28:20,020
Right. In those days, recorded videos were much more common than livestreams.

423
00:28:20,020 --> 00:28:21,820
Right, exactly.

424
00:28:21,820 --> 00:28:25,820
That's how I got into it. I had already been interested through videos and thought it looked appealing.

425
00:28:25,820 --> 00:28:26,820
Right, right.

426
00:28:26,820 --> 00:28:32,320
Watching those videos made me want to try it myself.

427
00:28:32,320 --> 00:28:39,660
But the technical side seemed far too difficult. You know, capture devices and everything.

428
00:28:39,660 --> 00:28:43,740
I wondered how anyone managed it. It felt like an unknown world.

429
00:28:43,740 --> 00:28:50,780
Even after I began streaming, I didn't understand capture devices for about a year.

430
00:28:50,780 --> 00:28:56,620
Really? I thought you were relatively good at using that equipment.

431
00:28:56,620 --> 00:29:03,220
I did quite a lot from the studio. Oh, so that's how you handled it at first.

432
00:29:03,220 --> 00:29:09,260
I did most of those early streams, including Amagami, from the studio.

433
00:29:09,260 --> 00:29:18,380
Really? That's unexpected. So when did you properly equip your home with a PC and everything?

434
00:29:18,380 --> 00:29:23,880
Honestly, probably about a year after I started.

435
00:29:23,880 --> 00:29:26,120
A whole year?

436
00:29:26,120 --> 00:29:26,600
Yes.

437
00:29:26,600 --> 00:29:38,560
Until then I commuted to the studio. There were fewer Livers, so I could quickly go to the office and stream.

438
00:29:38,560 --> 00:29:44,220
That's what I did. Though commuting every time sounds difficult too.

439
00:29:44,220 --> 00:29:51,600
I thought configuring all the equipment myself would be far harder.

440
00:29:51,600 --> 00:29:56,100
There is so much to learn before you get used to it. Exactly.

441
00:29:56,600 --> 00:30:08,560
So many tasks. At first I only played laptop-friendly Flash games and things like that. Actual game streaming—wow, that feels incredibly nostalgic now.

442
00:30:08,560 --> 00:30:16,500
I mostly watched commentary videos of romance games.

443
00:30:16,500 --> 00:30:22,520
Dating sims, gal games, and eroge. Did you watch action-game commentary, Lulu-chan?

444
00:30:22,520 --> 00:30:26,440
Yes, while you watched gal-game and eroge commentary, I watched action games.

445
00:30:26,440 --> 00:30:31,080
My favorite genre was horror.

446
00:30:31,080 --> 00:30:38,060
Really? Yes, partly because I like horror.

447
00:30:38,060 --> 00:30:46,940
Even when I play horror games, I get startled, but it usually doesn't show outwardly.

448
00:30:46,940 --> 00:30:53,560
That's true. Streamers often give entertaining reactions, don't they?

449
00:30:53,560 --> 00:30:56,240
Your perspective is a little frightening.

450
00:30:56,440 --> 00:31:04,880
Yes. I became addicted to watching people scream, so I kept seeking out horror streams.

451
00:31:04,880 --> 00:31:08,720
So that's how you enjoyed them. That's different from what I expected.

452
00:31:08,720 --> 00:31:15,380
For example, I watched your SIREN playthrough, Class Rep.

453
00:31:15,380 --> 00:31:18,340
I did play it. It was incredibly difficult.

454
00:31:18,340 --> 00:31:23,980
I watched it with a huge grin on my face.

455
00:31:23,980 --> 00:31:26,280
Scary! Yes.

456
00:31:26,280 --> 00:31:28,520
I didn't know people watched me from that perspective.

457
00:31:28,520 --> 00:31:31,620
I never told you, but I was watching.

458
00:31:31,620 --> 00:31:37,040
You know how some people calmly play horror games? Those were the ones I could watch.

459
00:31:37,040 --> 00:31:43,680
The opposite, then—you get scared yourself, so you want to watch someone calm.

460
00:31:43,680 --> 00:31:52,140
Right, the appeal reverses when the player doesn't get startled. I used to watch only people like that.

461
00:31:52,140 --> 00:31:56,120
So we enjoyed horror streams in opposite ways.

462
00:31:56,280 --> 00:32:01,820
Yes, completely opposite ways.

463
00:32:01,820 --> 00:32:04,320
I see. That's interesting.

464
00:32:04,320 --> 00:32:09,040
Here's another message related to games.

465
00:32:09,040 --> 00:32:11,560
All right. Here it is.

466
00:32:11,560 --> 00:32:16,160
“Konlulu, Lulu-chan and Class Rep.”

467
00:32:16,160 --> 00:32:24,520
“Suzuhara Lulu is strongly associated with long gaming streams, but did your family have any rules about games when you were young?

468
00:32:24,520 --> 00:32:35,080
“My household allowed only one hour a day, so I had to continue late at night after my parents fell asleep and spent my childhood chronically sleep-deprived.

469
00:32:35,080 --> 00:32:42,400
“The Class Rep has said that games were strictly prohibited in her childhood, which clearly turned her into the repressed high-school girl we see today.

470
00:32:42,400 --> 00:32:49,600
“Was Lulu-chan's household permissive about games, or were they so strict that you became absorbed in games later as a reaction?

471
00:32:49,600 --> 00:32:58,680
“Finally, I'm truly happy that Lulu-chan is back. The hot days continue, “but please keep streaming games without pushing yourself too hard.”

472
00:32:58,680 --> 00:33:01,520
Thank you.

473
00:33:01,520 --> 00:33:03,520
I'm curious about this one.

474
00:33:03,520 --> 00:33:15,440
You often hear that limiting children to one hour makes them overindulge once they're adults.

475
00:33:15,440 --> 00:33:17,440
How was it for you, Lulu-chan?

476
00:33:17,440 --> 00:33:22,380
In my case, there were absolutely no rules. It was lawless territory.

477
00:33:22,380 --> 00:33:24,380
Really?

478
00:33:24,380 --> 00:33:26,380
Absolutely no rules—a total free-for-all.

479
00:33:26,380 --> 00:33:27,380
None whatsoever.

480
00:33:27,380 --> 00:33:43,380
I'd heard that restricted children often compensate later, so I assumed children given complete freedom might lose interest in games. Apparently not.

481
00:33:43,380 --> 00:33:51,380
I've heard that too and wondered whether it was true, but it didn't apply to my family at all.

482
00:33:51,380 --> 00:33:52,380
I've been this way since I was little.

483
00:33:52,380 --> 00:33:53,380
Wow.

484
00:33:53,380 --> 00:34:01,380
I would play for hours and replay the same game endlessly.

485
00:34:01,380 --> 00:34:03,380
Really?

486
00:34:03,380 --> 00:34:05,380
What games did you play as a child?

487
00:34:05,380 --> 00:34:11,380
All sorts of things.

488
00:34:11,380 --> 00:34:12,380
Which ones really absorbed you?

489
00:34:12,380 --> 00:34:16,380
Online games, for one.

490
00:34:16,380 --> 00:34:17,380
Oh.

491
00:34:17,380 --> 00:34:19,380
I also played Monster Hunter.

492
00:34:19,380 --> 00:34:21,380
Right, I see.

493
00:34:21,380 --> 00:34:22,380
Mm-hmm.

494
00:34:22,380 --> 00:34:23,380
Wow.

495
00:34:23,380 --> 00:34:24,380
I see.

496
00:34:24,380 --> 00:34:30,380
We really may have been complete opposites.

497
00:34:30,380 --> 00:34:35,380
Then what was the first game you ever played, Class Rep?

498
00:34:35,380 --> 00:34:38,380
Animal Crossing.

499
00:34:38,380 --> 00:34:41,380
Animal Crossing was your first?

500
00:34:41,380 --> 00:34:54,380
Yes. My family genuinely wouldn't buy me games; they were prohibited. For some reason, computers were allowed, but I was limited to five minutes a day.

501
00:34:54,380 --> 00:34:55,380
Five minutes a day?

502
00:34:55,380 --> 00:35:00,380
I could use three five-minute vouchers, so effectively fifteen minutes.

503
00:35:00,380 --> 00:35:03,380
That's incredibly strict!

504
00:35:03,380 --> 00:35:05,380
It was strict.

505
00:35:05,380 --> 00:35:07,380
What can you possibly do in fifteen minutes?

506
00:35:07,380 --> 00:35:20,380
Exactly—nothing. Whenever I heard the common rule “one hour of games per day,” I thought, “That's so much! You get an entire hour?”

507
00:35:20,380 --> 00:35:25,380
Compared with five minutes, an hour certainly is long.

508
00:35:25,380 --> 00:35:26,380
Exactly.

509
00:35:26,380 --> 00:35:40,380
I'd use one five-minute voucher, launch a game, and then... walk a few steps before my time was over.

510
00:35:40,380 --> 00:35:41,380
Right.

511
00:35:41,380 --> 00:35:42,380
Especially in Animal Crossing.

512
00:35:42,380 --> 00:35:47,380
So on the computer I only played Flash minigames.

513
00:35:47,380 --> 00:35:49,380
Oh.

514
00:35:49,380 --> 00:35:53,380
The kind where one round genuinely ends within a minute.

515
00:35:53,380 --> 00:36:01,380
Even some card games can't be finished within five minutes.

516
00:36:01,380 --> 00:36:10,380
That's what I played. But when I was little, there were many times when neither parent was home.

517
00:36:10,380 --> 00:36:13,380
Then it was the Infinite Internet Arc.

518
00:36:13,380 --> 00:36:17,380
Oh! So they didn't monitor you that closely then?

519
00:36:17,380 --> 00:36:18,380
Right—the Infinite Internet Arc.

520
00:36:18,380 --> 00:36:19,380
Whenever they weren't home.

521
00:36:19,380 --> 00:36:20,380
I embarked on the Infinite Internet Arc.

522
00:36:20,380 --> 00:36:21,380
Mm-hmm.

523
00:36:21,380 --> 00:36:30,380
Games were finally permitted around fourth grade, because I obtained a game console through my own efforts.

524
00:36:30,380 --> 00:36:32,380
Did you save your allowance and buy it?

525
00:36:32,380 --> 00:36:35,380
There was a competition...

526
00:36:35,380 --> 00:36:36,380
A competition?

527
00:36:36,380 --> 00:36:38,380
A music competition.

528
00:36:38,380 --> 00:36:42,380
The prize for excellence was a Nintendo 3DS.

529
00:36:42,380 --> 00:36:45,380
Wow, that's an expensive prize!

530
00:36:45,380 --> 00:36:48,380
I won, so they had no choice but to let me have the 3DS.

531
00:36:48,380 --> 00:36:55,380
Then my parents said they'd buy me exactly one game in my entire life, and I chose Animal Crossing.

532
00:36:55,380 --> 00:37:00,380
That makes it especially memorable.

533
00:37:00,380 --> 00:37:02,380
It really does.

534
00:37:02,380 --> 00:37:09,380
What a story—you won a competition to obtain a console so you could play Animal Crossing.

535
00:37:09,380 --> 00:37:10,380
But still...

536
00:37:10,380 --> 00:37:15,380
With only that one game... there was no way I could be satisfied with a single game.

537
00:37:15,380 --> 00:37:16,380
Naturally.

538
00:37:16,380 --> 00:37:17,380
So...

539
00:37:17,380 --> 00:37:24,380
I slipped away to game shops behind my parents' backs, hiding while spending the allowance I'd saved.

540
00:37:24,380 --> 00:37:29,380
I bought games that had been discounted as far as possible.

541
00:37:29,380 --> 00:37:34,380
I kept nervously looking around while shopping, so they may have thought I was shoplifting.

542
00:37:34,380 --> 00:37:35,380
You must have looked incredibly suspicious.

543
00:37:35,380 --> 00:37:36,380
Exactly.

544
00:37:36,380 --> 00:37:45,380
But I'd desperately save my tiny allowance, and going to buy a game with it was incredibly exciting.

545
00:37:45,380 --> 00:37:46,380
It really was.

546
00:37:46,380 --> 00:37:47,380
So what did you buy?

547
00:37:47,380 --> 00:37:49,380
What came after Animal Crossing?

548
00:37:49,380 --> 00:37:55,380
Games like 7th Dragon, and only Ace Attorney 4.

549
00:37:55,380 --> 00:37:56,380
Wow.

550
00:37:56,380 --> 00:38:02,380
Somehow... that already sounds very much like you, Class Rep.

551
00:38:02,380 --> 00:38:13,380
This isn't specifically criticism of Ace Attorney, but I could afford only the entries in a series that weren't particularly well received.

552
00:38:13,380 --> 00:38:16,380
Because those were heavily discounted.

553
00:38:16,380 --> 00:38:21,380
That makes sense. Popular games rarely get marked down.

554
00:38:21,380 --> 00:38:22,380
Exactly.

555
00:38:22,380 --> 00:38:24,380
That's how it was.

556
00:38:24,380 --> 00:38:25,380
I see.

557
00:38:25,380 --> 00:38:29,380
I really envied households where games were a free-for-all.

558
00:38:29,380 --> 00:38:39,380
Hearing that, I realize I did practically nothing but play games.

559
00:38:39,380 --> 00:38:42,380
Maybe your parents trusted you.

560
00:38:42,380 --> 00:38:44,380
Or perhaps they'd simply given up on me.

561
00:38:44,380 --> 00:38:45,380
Given up?

562
00:38:45,380 --> 00:38:46,380
Really?

563
00:38:46,380 --> 00:38:53,380
Maybe they assumed I wouldn't listen even if they told me to stop.

564
00:38:53,380 --> 00:38:55,380
I see.

565
00:38:55,380 --> 00:38:59,380
Perhaps your parents liked games too, Lulu-chan.

566
00:38:59,380 --> 00:39:02,380
Oh, that may have made a big difference.

567
00:39:02,380 --> 00:39:03,380
True.

568
00:39:03,380 --> 00:39:04,380
That could be it.

569
00:39:04,380 --> 00:39:07,380
Did you ever play games with your parents?

570
00:39:07,380 --> 00:39:12,380
I would watch my parents play.

571
00:39:12,380 --> 00:39:14,380
Oh, really?

572
00:39:14,380 --> 00:39:15,380
Mm-hmm.

573
00:39:15,380 --> 00:39:18,380
You have siblings, don't you?

574
00:39:18,380 --> 00:39:19,380
I do.

575
00:39:19,380 --> 00:39:23,380
Do they love games too?

576
00:39:23,380 --> 00:39:24,380
Not at all.

577
00:39:24,380 --> 00:39:26,380
Oh, really?

578
00:39:26,380 --> 00:39:33,380
I have an older sister, but she isn't much of a gamer.

579
00:39:33,380 --> 00:39:34,380
I see.

580
00:39:34,380 --> 00:39:43,380
That's probably my fault. I loved games so much as a child, and naturally my sister was chosen as my playmate.

581
00:39:43,380 --> 00:39:44,380
Right, right.

582
00:39:44,380 --> 00:39:47,380
I constantly begged her, “Let's play! Again! One more time!”

583
00:39:47,380 --> 00:39:48,380
You haven't changed at all!

584
00:39:48,380 --> 00:40:02,380
Apparently it traumatized her. Even when I brought over a board game, she'd flee at incredible speed.

585
00:40:02,380 --> 00:40:03,380
[Laughter]

586
00:40:03,380 --> 00:40:04,380
That's what she became like.

587
00:40:04,380 --> 00:40:06,380
Wow—you truly haven't changed.

588
00:40:06,380 --> 00:40:08,380
She stopped playing with me.

589
00:40:08,380 --> 00:40:13,380
Maybe you were emitting an incredibly sinister aura.

590
00:40:13,380 --> 00:40:14,380
[Laughter]

591
00:40:14,380 --> 00:40:16,380
“Big sister, let's play one more time!”

592
00:40:16,380 --> 00:40:20,380
“One more time! Big sister, one more time!”

593
00:40:20,380 --> 00:40:28,380
I challenged her endlessly, so I probably traumatized her. Looking back, I feel sorry about it.

594
00:40:28,380 --> 00:40:34,380
I see. That's such a great story—I'm delighted.

595
00:40:34,380 --> 00:40:35,380
You haven't changed.

596
00:40:35,380 --> 00:40:40,380
It taught me that I have to be careful when playing with other people.

597
00:40:40,380 --> 00:40:41,380
Mm-hmm.

598
00:40:41,380 --> 00:40:42,380
That may be why something new developed from it.

599
00:40:42,380 --> 00:40:44,920
Maybe that's why I mostly play solo now.

600
00:40:44,920 --> 00:40:47,820
I see, I see.

601
00:40:47,820 --> 00:40:54,660
It's fascinating to hear how Lulu-chan's personality was formed.

602
00:40:54,660 --> 00:40:56,960
I genuinely haven't changed.

603
00:40:56,960 --> 00:41:01,500
After hearing that, “the child is father to the adult” really fits.

604
00:41:01,500 --> 00:41:06,340
Speaking of childhood, I'm really curious about yours, Class Rep.

605
00:41:06,340 --> 00:41:12,260
I've always imagined you as extremely active, as someone who takes initiative in all sorts of things.

606
00:41:12,260 --> 00:41:15,120
When did that begin? Were you always like that?

607
00:41:15,120 --> 00:41:26,420
On days without school—weekends and such—I could choose whether to go play with friends, right?

608
00:41:26,420 --> 00:41:27,920
Right.

609
00:41:27,920 --> 00:41:35,300
But if none of my friends were available, I'd have no plans for the weekend.

610
00:41:35,300 --> 00:41:41,460
I remember sobbing and crying, “I'm bored!”

611
00:41:42,260 --> 00:41:48,800
I absolutely hated being bored as a child.

612
00:41:48,800 --> 00:41:51,500
So you always wanted to play...

613
00:41:51,500 --> 00:41:53,580
You needed your schedule constantly filled?

614
00:41:53,580 --> 00:41:58,100
I had three childhood friends besides myself.

615
00:41:58,100 --> 00:42:07,020
They were my only friends, so if none were free, I'd sob and scream, “I'm bored!”

616
00:42:07,020 --> 00:42:09,460
That's adorable!

617
00:42:09,460 --> 00:42:09,760
Yes.

618
00:42:09,760 --> 00:42:12,100
There was a reason I was crying so hard!

619
00:42:12,260 --> 00:42:13,260
Right.

620
00:42:13,260 --> 00:42:16,580
That's how I was.

621
00:42:16,580 --> 00:42:22,340
Even as an adult, you do seem to keep your schedule constantly packed.

622
00:42:22,340 --> 00:42:32,360
Now I have the internet whenever I need it, so I no longer cry about being bored.

623
00:42:32,360 --> 00:42:40,100
If the internet died, I'd revert immediately: “I'm bored! Waaah!”

624
00:42:40,100 --> 00:42:41,380
So you can't live without the internet.

625
00:42:41,380 --> 00:42:42,100
Back then I thought having something to do was really important.

626
00:42:42,260 --> 00:42:44,260
I only had fifteen minutes of internet access.

627
00:42:44,260 --> 00:42:46,260
Right.

628
00:42:46,260 --> 00:42:48,260
There genuinely was nothing to do.

629
00:42:48,260 --> 00:42:55,260
These days, living without the internet certainly would be difficult.

630
00:42:55,260 --> 00:42:58,260
I was an extremely hyperactive child.

631
00:42:58,260 --> 00:43:00,260
A hyperactive kid!

632
00:43:00,260 --> 00:43:08,260
I'd like to see you deprived of the entire internet, terrifying as that sounds.

633
00:43:08,260 --> 00:43:10,260
It would be an interesting experiment.

634
00:43:10,260 --> 00:43:11,260
Oh no.

635
00:43:11,260 --> 00:43:12,260
Maybe while middle-school students are studying...

636
00:43:12,260 --> 00:43:15,260
While you're studying, you could shut yourself away in the mountains.

637
00:43:15,260 --> 00:43:16,260
Ah.

638
00:43:16,260 --> 00:43:21,260
I'd like to see you survive alone and find out what withdrawal symptoms emerge.

639
00:43:21,260 --> 00:43:26,260
I might manage in the mountains. I desperately wanted to live in the countryside.

640
00:43:26,260 --> 00:43:32,260
As a bored child, I thought cities were places where you couldn't do anything without money.

641
00:43:32,260 --> 00:43:40,260
Maybe I could manage in the countryside. I feel like I'd only start crying once both money and internet were taken away.

642
00:43:40,260 --> 00:43:42,640
Maybe so—in a city.

643
00:43:42,640 --> 00:43:46,260
I see. Shall we buy some land together, then?

644
00:43:46,260 --> 00:43:53,260
I'd like that—setting the previous discussion aside.

645
00:43:53,260 --> 00:43:57,060
Let's establish Mount Mito-Lulu! We could even invite Nijisanji Livers.

646
00:43:57,060 --> 00:44:01,260
That could work. Everyone could pool money for some mountain land.

647
00:44:01,260 --> 00:44:06,140
We'd buy a mountain and do whatever we wanted there. Doesn't that sound fun?

648
00:44:06,140 --> 00:44:13,140
Serious hobbyists apparently buy mountains for airsoft or camping.

649
00:44:13,140 --> 00:44:27,640
It genuinely sounds fun. I'm interested in building our own houses and doing all sorts of things ourselves.

650
00:44:27,640 --> 00:44:31,020
True. Could we create an entire town?

651
00:44:31,020 --> 00:44:34,680
We could build Niji Town!

652
00:44:34,680 --> 00:44:36,120
Niji Town, Block One—for real.

653
00:44:36,120 --> 00:44:38,120
You mean I could actually live there? Wonderful!

654
00:44:38,120 --> 00:44:43,120
I'll be the caretaker, since it could be dangerous.

655
00:44:43,120 --> 00:44:45,120
That sounds fantastic.

656
00:44:45,120 --> 00:44:52,120
Shall we make our own DASH Village? We might seriously consider it.

657
00:44:52,120 --> 00:44:56,120
If we asked other Livers, more of them might be interested than we'd expect.

658
00:44:56,120 --> 00:44:59,120
Let's build a retirement home.

659
00:44:59,120 --> 00:45:04,120
Livers actually talk about that quite often.

660
00:45:04,120 --> 00:45:05,120
It comes up in our line of work.

661
00:45:05,120 --> 00:45:16,120
Because of our profession, we say that once we're old, we should build a retirement home together and all live there.

662
00:45:16,120 --> 00:45:17,120
I hate that you discuss this so often!

663
00:45:17,120 --> 00:45:22,120
But our mountain could do that too.

664
00:45:22,120 --> 00:45:23,120
You really talk about it that often?

665
00:45:23,120 --> 00:45:32,120
We could watch out for each other, so nobody suddenly goes silent in the middle of a stream.

666
00:45:32,120 --> 00:45:33,120
Some retirement homes even have an internal currency, apparently.

667
00:45:33,120 --> 00:45:36,520
Yes, their own currency inside the facility.

668
00:45:36,520 --> 00:45:37,500
Like perica.

669
00:45:37,500 --> 00:45:41,720
You earn money inside the facility by doing rehabilitation exercises.

670
00:45:41,720 --> 00:45:43,480
Places like that exist?

671
00:45:43,480 --> 00:45:47,340
Yes. We could make Niji Coins and create a real economy.

672
00:45:47,340 --> 00:45:49,560
That actually sounds interesting.

673
00:45:49,560 --> 00:45:51,740
The possibilities keep expanding.

674
00:45:51,740 --> 00:45:58,800
We'd create a currency and earn it as rewards.

675
00:45:58,800 --> 00:46:03,800
Let's use acorns as our currency.

676
00:46:03,800 --> 00:46:06,320
That could be fun.

677
00:46:06,320 --> 00:46:10,140
Let's move on to the next message.

678
00:46:10,140 --> 00:46:15,100
Maybe this one.

679
00:46:15,100 --> 00:46:21,180
“Hello and good evening, Class Rep and Lulu-chan. Konlulu!

680
00:46:21,180 --> 00:46:28,580
“Getting straight to the question: in the Class Rep's opinion, “which Nijisanji Liver would be interesting “to collaborate with Lulu-chan?

681
00:46:28,800 --> 00:46:35,120
“I expect you might answer yourself, “but I'd appreciate someone who debuted after Lulu-chan graduated.”

682
00:46:35,120 --> 00:46:38,400
That's the question.

683
00:46:38,400 --> 00:46:40,600
From Natsumikan-san.

684
00:46:40,600 --> 00:46:43,180
Thank you.

685
00:46:43,180 --> 00:46:47,260
I am the first choice, naturally—so we're choosing the second.

686
00:46:47,260 --> 00:46:50,060
Exactly.

687
00:46:50,060 --> 00:46:51,300
The second choice.

688
00:46:51,300 --> 00:46:58,800
Come to think of it, Lulu-chan, you didn't collaborate with that many Livers before graduating, did you?

689
00:46:58,800 --> 00:47:02,400
Not really.

690
00:47:02,400 --> 00:47:04,280
So there are many possibilities.

691
00:47:04,280 --> 00:47:07,360
You actually did hardly any collaborations.

692
00:47:07,440 --> 00:47:10,560
Probably not.

693
00:47:10,560 --> 00:47:12,980
That's why Lulu-chan has enormous potential here.

694
00:47:12,980 --> 00:47:15,840
I'm not sure, though.

695
00:47:15,840 --> 00:47:18,800
I don't think I'm particularly suited to collaborations.

696
00:47:18,800 --> 00:47:24,560
No, collaboration skills are something you cultivate.

697
00:47:24,800 --> 00:47:25,360
I see.

698
00:47:25,360 --> 00:47:27,860
Some people are naturally suited to them, but you also become accustomed to them by doing more.

699
00:47:27,860 --> 00:47:34,020
I was much worse when I started.

700
00:47:34,020 --> 00:47:47,880
No, you're very charming! I don't know how suited I am now either, but you should seriously watch the earliest JK-gumi streams. They're terrible.

701
00:47:47,880 --> 00:47:53,000
I did watch them, and you spoke perfectly normally.

702
00:47:53,000 --> 00:48:01,500
From my perspective, being able to talk normally already means you have aptitude. I feel like we're talking normally now.

703
00:48:01,500 --> 00:48:06,440
Still, how should I put it...

704
00:48:06,440 --> 00:48:15,560
Maybe because I haven't collaborated much, I'm not someone who speaks up often.

705
00:48:15,560 --> 00:48:22,980
In larger groups, you have to actively contribute much more, you know?

706
00:48:22,980 --> 00:48:24,980
I see.

707
00:48:24,980 --> 00:48:28,980
That is unavoidable in a large group.

708
00:48:28,980 --> 00:48:34,480
Then I'll decide who Lulu-chan collaborates with. Please do!

709
00:48:34,480 --> 00:48:39,680
Who should you collaborate with? “Should” sounds a little odd, but who is the best match?

710
00:48:39,680 --> 00:48:51,600
The listener specified someone who debuted after you graduated, but if we temporarily remove that restriction... Tell me if anyone comes to mind.

711
00:48:51,600 --> 00:48:54,900
First, Hayama-san.

712
00:48:54,900 --> 00:48:56,900
Ah!

713
00:48:56,900 --> 00:49:01,900
Hayama Marin. Her soul is shaped like Lulu-chan's.

714
00:49:01,900 --> 00:49:04,900
You're similar.

715
00:49:04,900 --> 00:49:10,900
I'd really like to see the two of you play a game together for hours.

716
00:49:10,900 --> 00:49:15,900
I agree completely.

717
00:49:15,900 --> 00:49:22,820
I've actually wanted to play games and talk with Marin-chan.

718
00:49:22,820 --> 00:49:23,820
That sounds good.

719
00:49:23,820 --> 00:49:35,820
I visited one of her call-in streams and played with her briefly, but I'd like to do something longer together.

720
00:49:35,820 --> 00:49:49,820
Hayama-san also often posts on Twitter with her eyes spinning, which is great. I'd like to witness infinity multiplied by infinity.

721
00:49:49,820 --> 00:49:51,820
I'm also curious about Debidebi Debiru.

722
00:49:51,820 --> 00:50:00,820
For an unpredictable chemical reaction, Debiru and Fumino Tamaki both interest me.

723
00:50:00,820 --> 00:50:08,820
I actually predicted that you'd say Fumino Tamaki-san.

724
00:50:08,820 --> 00:50:10,820
Though she didn't debut after I left.

725
00:50:10,820 --> 00:50:11,820
Right.

726
00:50:11,820 --> 00:50:13,820
Not after I graduated.

727
00:50:13,820 --> 00:50:14,820
Exactly.

728
00:50:14,820 --> 00:50:19,820
I thought you'd say you wanted me to collaborate with Fumino Tamaki-san.

729
00:50:19,820 --> 00:50:20,820
Of course I do.

730
00:50:20,820 --> 00:50:21,820
Mm-hmm.

731
00:50:21,820 --> 00:50:22,820
Absolutely.

732
00:50:22,820 --> 00:50:25,820
I want to see Fumino Tamaki have a one-on-one collaboration with everyone.

733
00:50:25,820 --> 00:50:29,820
I'd like to see that too.

734
00:50:29,820 --> 00:50:31,820
And Mashiro-kun.

735
00:50:33,820 --> 00:50:37,820
Mashiro-kun's soul is shaped somewhat like yours too.

736
00:50:37,820 --> 00:50:42,820
I've personally watched some of his streams.

737
00:50:42,820 --> 00:50:43,820
I thought so.

738
00:50:43,820 --> 00:50:45,820
They're interesting.

739
00:50:45,820 --> 00:50:46,820
Mm-hmm.

740
00:50:46,820 --> 00:50:50,820
Mashiro-kun is truly remarkable.

741
00:50:50,820 --> 00:50:57,820
He treats everyone the same, right, and his pupils are always dilated.

742
00:50:57,820 --> 00:51:05,820
Exactly. I wondered whether we might have something in common.

743
00:51:05,820 --> 00:51:06,820
Perhaps.

744
00:51:06,820 --> 00:51:09,820
He likes occult and horror subjects too.

745
00:51:09,820 --> 00:51:10,820
Right.

746
00:51:10,820 --> 00:51:13,820
And he visits all sorts of haunted places.

747
00:51:13,820 --> 00:51:14,820
Mm-hmm.

748
00:51:14,820 --> 00:51:17,820
I watched bits of that and thought it was amazing.

749
00:51:17,820 --> 00:51:18,820
Right.

750
00:51:18,820 --> 00:51:21,820
I've watched him here and there.

751
00:51:21,820 --> 00:51:28,820
He follows his own path, so I think you'd be quite compatible.

752
00:51:28,820 --> 00:51:32,820
And simply, Yang Nari as well.

753
00:51:32,820 --> 00:51:33,820
Ah.

754
00:51:33,820 --> 00:51:39,820
She's cute and you're cute, so I simply want to see cute times cute.

755
00:51:39,820 --> 00:51:50,640
Everyone you named is someone I've been interested in, so I'm amazed by how accurate your choices are.

756
00:51:50,640 --> 00:51:55,780
I think you mentioned her before, but have you collaborated with Sophia-san yet?

757
00:51:57,040 --> 00:52:08,060
We appeared together briefly on an official program, but we haven't done a substantial one-on-one collaboration.

758
00:52:08,060 --> 00:52:14,820
Maybe you haven't initiated many collaborations yourself, Lulu-chan?

759
00:52:16,540 --> 00:52:16,800
No.

760
00:52:16,800 --> 00:52:17,980
You haven't?

761
00:52:18,940 --> 00:52:19,880
I might not have.

762
00:52:19,880 --> 00:52:23,640
I'd like you to try producing a collaboration yourself.

763
00:52:23,640 --> 00:52:27,840
Yes, I think I'd like to.

764
00:52:27,840 --> 00:52:32,300
I'll even compose the Discord message for you.

765
00:52:32,300 --> 00:52:39,380
Really? If you'll go that far, perhaps you should participate too.

766
00:52:39,380 --> 00:52:41,160
No, no.

767
00:52:41,160 --> 00:52:45,060
I'll help bring it about, but I want to watch Lulu-chan have a one-on-one collaboration with someone.

768
00:52:45,060 --> 00:52:46,100
A one-on-one collaboration.

769
00:52:46,100 --> 00:52:47,100
Exactly.

770
00:52:47,100 --> 00:52:52,960
Come to think of it, I may never have invited anyone myself.

771
00:52:52,960 --> 00:53:00,540
Lulu-chan's return must have meaning.

772
00:53:00,540 --> 00:53:08,440
Doing things you never did before—that is the revelation here.

773
00:53:08,440 --> 00:53:10,640
From a spiritual perspective.

774
00:53:10,640 --> 00:53:11,120
That's true.

775
00:53:11,120 --> 00:53:12,020
Exactly.

776
00:53:12,020 --> 00:53:19,800
Since returning, I have mm-hmm, mm-hmm, increased the number of collaborations I do.

777
00:53:19,800 --> 00:53:24,660
Right. You've even participated in tournaments.

778
00:53:24,660 --> 00:53:26,660
I'd actually never done that before.

779
00:53:26,660 --> 00:53:27,900
Mm-hmm.

780
00:53:27,900 --> 00:53:33,500
I'd participated in sponsored events, but not ordinary tournaments.

781
00:53:33,500 --> 00:53:40,700
Since returning, I joined an internal Nijisanji tournament for the first time.

782
00:53:40,700 --> 00:53:43,100
I think that's wonderful.

783
00:53:43,100 --> 00:53:45,220
Really wonderful.

784
00:53:45,220 --> 00:53:48,720
Honestly, mm-hmm, once I tried it, it was fun.

785
00:53:48,720 --> 00:53:49,040
Right.

786
00:53:49,040 --> 00:54:00,420
It had felt like such a high hurdle, mm-hmm, but after trying it, I realized I didn't need to be so nervous or intimidated.

787
00:54:00,420 --> 00:54:01,560
Exactly.

788
00:54:01,560 --> 00:54:02,540
That's how I've begun to feel.

789
00:54:02,540 --> 00:54:09,300
Especially since you like online games, I'd love to see more of that.

790
00:54:09,300 --> 00:54:15,100
Yes. From here on, I want to be more active and challenge myself.

791
00:54:15,100 --> 00:54:15,240
Mm-hmm.

792
00:54:15,240 --> 00:54:22,760
This year—or rather, since I've been fortunate enough to return, I want to talk with many different people.

793
00:54:22,760 --> 00:54:23,480
Right.

794
00:54:23,480 --> 00:54:27,120
I want to be proactive.

795
00:54:27,120 --> 00:54:29,780
That may be extremely important.

796
00:54:29,780 --> 00:54:30,620
Mm-hmm.

797
00:54:30,620 --> 00:54:32,540
That makes me happy.

798
00:54:32,540 --> 00:54:38,440
I'm glad. It makes me happy that you'd even compose the message for me.

799
00:54:38,440 --> 00:54:40,320
Of course I will.

800
00:54:40,320 --> 00:54:41,780
I've done it plenty of times.

801
00:54:41,780 --> 00:54:42,820
I'm delighted. I'll do it!

802
00:54:42,820 --> 00:54:48,920
I have templates I've sent to numerous people, so I only need to make a few changes.

803
00:54:48,920 --> 00:54:53,220
A template! I would definitely like that.

804
00:54:53,220 --> 00:54:56,020
Please, use it.

805
00:54:56,020 --> 00:54:56,580
Then...

806
00:54:56,580 --> 00:55:00,740
There's also the idol festival in December.

807
00:55:00,740 --> 00:55:02,500
Right, the idol festival.

808
00:55:02,500 --> 00:55:10,960
The Class Rep and I appear on different days—Day 1 and Day 2—yes, different days, but I'll be working with some people for the first time.

809
00:55:10,960 --> 00:55:14,920
Right, so before then...

810
00:55:14,920 --> 00:55:15,620
Mm-hmm.

811
00:55:15,620 --> 00:55:22,220
We haven't had many opportunities to meet yet. Have rehearsals begun?

812
00:55:22,220 --> 00:55:25,860
Not yet. I think they're still ahead of us.

813
00:55:25,860 --> 00:55:28,420
I'm looking forward to what comes next.

814
00:55:28,420 --> 00:55:34,080
Spending lots of time together in rehearsals really helps people become close.

815
00:55:34,080 --> 00:55:37,800
Exactly. That's what I'm looking forward to.

816
00:55:37,800 --> 00:55:38,660
I'm excited.

817
00:55:38,660 --> 00:55:39,860
I'm really looking forward to rehearsals.

818
00:55:39,860 --> 00:55:42,500
Me too.

819
00:55:42,500 --> 00:55:44,740
Let's move to the next message.

820
00:55:44,740 --> 00:55:45,460
Okay.

821
00:55:45,620 --> 00:55:49,020
This one interests me too.

822
00:55:49,020 --> 00:55:54,840
“What was the most enjoyable entertainment or experience Suzuhara Lulu discovered after graduating?

823
00:55:54,840 --> 00:56:00,540
“And if the Class Rep graduated, what would she like to try that she cannot do now?”

824
00:56:00,540 --> 00:56:03,940
From Mito Radio listener Snow-san. Thank you.

825
00:56:03,940 --> 00:56:05,500
Thank you.

826
00:56:05,500 --> 00:56:10,840
I am curious what you were doing then.

827
00:56:10,840 --> 00:56:12,840
Let's see...

828
00:56:12,840 --> 00:56:17,540
I was doing various things —mm-hmm—in the demon realm.

829
00:56:17,540 --> 00:56:17,840
Right.

830
00:56:17,840 --> 00:56:20,800
Was there anything you particularly enjoyed or became absorbed in?

831
00:56:20,800 --> 00:56:23,940
As for something I enjoyed or got into...

832
00:56:23,940 --> 00:56:24,120
Mm-hmm.

833
00:56:24,120 --> 00:56:37,600
Before graduating, I had never —right—gone to see a theater company or stage production.

834
00:56:37,600 --> 00:56:38,100
Really?

835
00:56:38,100 --> 00:56:39,300
Mm-hmm.

836
00:56:39,300 --> 00:56:42,460
For the first time, I went to see productions by the Shiki Theatre Company.

837
00:56:42,460 --> 00:56:43,620
Oh!

838
00:56:43,620 --> 00:56:50,020
I went to see them, mm-hmm, and the experience was so new that it surprised me.

839
00:56:50,020 --> 00:56:50,860
That's wonderful.

840
00:56:50,860 --> 00:56:52,760
It was really interesting.

841
00:56:52,760 --> 00:56:54,580
What did you see?

842
00:56:54,580 --> 00:56:59,720
Aladdin and The Lion King.

843
00:56:59,720 --> 00:57:00,500
Oh!

844
00:57:00,500 --> 00:57:01,740
I went to see those.

845
00:57:01,740 --> 00:57:02,500
Right.

846
00:57:02,500 --> 00:57:08,400
I'd always been curious, but had never gone before.

847
00:57:08,400 --> 00:57:15,460
That was my first exposure to what makes theater special, and it felt very fresh.

848
00:57:15,460 --> 00:57:18,120
Did it make you want to perform yourself?

849
00:57:18,120 --> 00:57:22,700
Performing still seems rather difficult.

850
00:57:22,700 --> 00:57:23,740
Right.

851
00:57:23,740 --> 00:57:25,700
That's how I feel.

852
00:57:25,700 --> 00:57:27,700
Theater is nice.

853
00:57:27,700 --> 00:57:28,220
I also...

854
00:57:28,220 --> 00:57:29,540
Do you attend plays?

855
00:57:29,540 --> 00:57:33,640
I've probably been fewer than ten times.

856
00:57:33,640 --> 00:57:36,340
That's still a fair number.

857
00:57:36,340 --> 00:57:39,820
I often watched high-school theater.

858
00:57:39,820 --> 00:57:40,360
Oh.

859
00:57:40,360 --> 00:57:42,460
Some of my friends performed in it.

860
00:57:42,460 --> 00:57:43,500
Mm-hmm.

861
00:57:43,500 --> 00:57:58,040
So I watched quite a lot of it, but high-school theater is, well... you know... how should I put it... the sound equipment isn't always very good, which is understandable,

862
00:57:58,040 --> 00:58:02,520
and the material itself can be so-so.

863
00:58:02,520 --> 00:58:11,780
Since I began by watching my friends' productions, I assumed that was simply what theater was like.

864
00:58:11,780 --> 00:58:20,660
But once I began seeing professional adult productions, I remember thinking, “Oh, this is fun!”

865
00:58:20,660 --> 00:58:24,560
Still, getting to watch a friend act is a rare opportunity.

866
00:58:24,560 --> 00:58:25,320
Only because they were involved.

867
00:58:25,320 --> 00:58:27,760
Otherwise I would never have seen it.

868
00:58:27,760 --> 00:58:29,860
Exactly.

869
00:58:29,860 --> 00:58:38,260
Speaking of that, I'd love to see the Class Rep in a serious stage play or musical.

870
00:58:38,260 --> 00:58:41,440
A musical does interest me.

871
00:58:41,440 --> 00:58:42,420
Sometimes in real life, you suddenly feel like singing, don't you?

872
00:58:42,420 --> 00:58:45,420
You suddenly want to sing.

873
00:58:45,440 --> 00:58:48,240
Oh, a musical in real life.

874
00:58:48,240 --> 00:58:50,320
If I suddenly sang in real life, people would look at me very strangely.

875
00:58:50,320 --> 00:58:53,940
But some nights you suddenly want to sing.

876
00:58:53,940 --> 00:58:57,220
So I would like to try it.

877
00:58:57,220 --> 00:59:03,440
A Nijisanji musical would be enormous, with so many people.

878
00:59:03,440 --> 00:59:05,400
Exactly.

879
00:59:05,400 --> 00:59:08,460
I'd be willing to do something on the scale of a school play.

880
00:59:08,460 --> 00:59:09,560
A school play?

881
00:59:09,560 --> 00:59:10,660
Seriously?

882
00:59:10,660 --> 00:59:12,820
You mean something like playing seaweed in a school play?

883
00:59:12,820 --> 00:59:15,320
The kind where every student is forcibly assigned a role?

884
00:59:15,440 --> 00:59:17,100
Like elementary school.

885
00:59:17,100 --> 00:59:29,320
But if you had a production with heroines and major characters, right, what kind of role would you want?

886
00:59:29,320 --> 00:59:33,280
Obviously, the mean girl.

887
00:59:33,280 --> 00:59:34,320
The mean girl?

888
00:59:34,320 --> 00:59:42,240
You know—the nasty, high-status girl common in foreign dramas.

889
00:59:42,240 --> 00:59:43,140
I want to play her.

890
00:59:43,140 --> 00:59:44,080
The one who bullies people?

891
00:59:44,080 --> 00:59:45,280
Exactly.

892
00:59:45,440 --> 00:59:46,940
Bullying the newcomer.

893
00:59:46,940 --> 00:59:48,060
Exactly.

894
00:59:48,060 --> 00:59:51,460
I understand. That sounds fun.

895
00:59:51,460 --> 00:59:53,320
It sounds incredibly fun.

896
00:59:53,320 --> 00:59:54,080
It does.

897
00:59:54,080 --> 00:59:55,100
I'd love to do that.

898
00:59:55,100 --> 01:00:00,000
A villain role, yes. I'd rather play someone bad too—even in a school play.

899
01:00:00,000 --> 01:00:04,200
That sounds great. I'm curious; I'd genuinely like to see it.

900
01:00:04,200 --> 01:00:09,920
It sounds entertaining. I want Nijisanji to do one.

901
01:00:09,920 --> 01:00:13,980
What surprised me about professional theater was the stage machinery.

902
01:00:13,980 --> 01:00:15,280
I understand! Exactly!

903
01:00:15,280 --> 01:00:20,740
There were so many moments when I wondered, “How did they do that?”

904
01:00:20,740 --> 01:00:24,480
That may have impressed me most.

905
01:00:24,480 --> 01:00:26,900
Absolutely.

906
01:00:26,900 --> 01:00:35,600
The productions are elaborate, and you see mechanisms live that you could never imagine.

907
01:00:35,600 --> 01:00:41,600
It creates a different sense of presence from video.

908
01:00:41,600 --> 01:00:53,240
As for what I'd want to do if I graduated—something I can't do now—I don't think Lulu-chan did this,

909
01:00:53,240 --> 01:00:56,640
but: a graduation concert.

910
01:00:58,120 --> 01:01:02,200
A 3D graduation concert.

911
01:01:02,200 --> 01:01:07,660
I would really like to do one.

912
01:01:07,660 --> 01:01:10,260
True—a graduation concert.

913
01:01:10,260 --> 01:01:18,720
Speaking of that, part of me wanted to do one too, so I understand.

914
01:01:18,720 --> 01:01:20,240
A graduation concert.

915
01:01:20,240 --> 01:01:30,060
When I listen to songs, I often think, “This would be perfect to sing at a graduation concert.”

916
01:01:30,060 --> 01:01:32,580
You do imagine it sometimes.

917
01:01:32,580 --> 01:01:34,300
Exactly.

918
01:01:34,300 --> 01:01:39,120
There are songs you can only sing when graduating.

919
01:01:39,120 --> 01:01:41,380
Only at that moment.

920
01:01:41,380 --> 01:01:46,700
I thought, why not hold one even without plans to graduate?

921
01:01:46,700 --> 01:01:48,420
Like holding your own funeral while you're alive.

922
01:01:48,420 --> 01:01:50,500
Call it a graduation concert.

923
01:01:50,500 --> 01:01:51,820
Exactly.

924
01:01:51,820 --> 01:01:54,100
It doesn't have to mean graduating from Nijisanji.

925
01:01:54,100 --> 01:01:54,600
Right.

926
01:01:54,600 --> 01:01:57,760
You could announce that you're graduating from something else.

927
01:01:57,760 --> 01:02:00,240
True. That's a good idea.

928
01:02:00,240 --> 01:02:00,820
For example...

929
01:02:00,820 --> 01:02:03,660
“Starting today, I am graduating from...”

930
01:02:03,660 --> 01:02:08,100
Yes? wearing shoes whenever I go outside.

931
01:02:08,100 --> 01:02:10,380
You're practically ceasing to be human!

932
01:02:10,380 --> 01:02:11,220
Graduating from wearing shoes outdoors would certainly qualify.

933
01:02:11,220 --> 01:02:19,300
I have a habit of picking at my nails after a bath, so perhaps I could hold a “graduating from nail-picking” concert.

934
01:02:19,300 --> 01:02:21,620
You'd have to put down your nail at the end.

935
01:02:21,620 --> 01:02:22,100
Like putting down the microphone.

936
01:02:22,100 --> 01:02:24,960
That's frightening.

937
01:02:24,960 --> 01:02:29,220
You sound like someone from the underworld.

938
01:02:29,220 --> 01:02:31,980
But using a trivial graduation like that would be ideal.

939
01:02:31,980 --> 01:02:33,460
Exactly.

940
01:02:33,460 --> 01:02:43,700
After all, before you actually graduate, there's always a chance of an accident or even dying, so you might never get to hold a graduation concert.

941
01:02:43,700 --> 01:02:46,820
True. You never know what may happen.

942
01:02:46,820 --> 01:02:49,140
A graduation concert is absolutely worthwhile.

943
01:02:49,140 --> 01:02:52,580
Doing one without plans to graduate sounds valid.

944
01:02:52,580 --> 01:02:55,120
I'd like to see that.

945
01:02:55,120 --> 01:03:07,740
When it really is someone's graduation, I've watched people hold graduation concerts, and they're always sad.

946
01:03:07,740 --> 01:03:08,660
Sad.

947
01:03:08,660 --> 01:03:10,060
They really are.

948
01:03:10,220 --> 01:03:11,700
And lonely.

949
01:03:11,700 --> 01:03:14,340
They're sad, but I still love them somehow.

950
01:03:14,340 --> 01:03:16,820
You keep thinking, “Please don't let this end.”

951
01:03:16,820 --> 01:03:22,380
And then you realize, “So this is the final song they've chosen.”

952
01:03:22,380 --> 01:03:25,220
It really hurts.

953
01:03:25,220 --> 01:03:27,560
I'll invite you to my graduation concert, then.

954
01:03:27,560 --> 01:03:28,140
Lulu-chan.

955
01:03:28,140 --> 01:03:32,380
Yes, please invite me to the graduation concert where you don't graduate.

956
01:03:32,380 --> 01:03:35,100
Absolutely.

957
01:03:35,100 --> 01:03:37,220
I'm genuinely curious. I want to do it—let's do it.

958
01:03:37,220 --> 01:03:38,660
That does sound good.

959
01:03:38,660 --> 01:03:39,340
Let's do it.

960
01:03:40,220 --> 01:03:44,700
There really are streams you can only make at a particular moment.

961
01:03:44,700 --> 01:03:45,460
Right.

962
01:03:45,460 --> 01:03:47,060
Those opportunities exist.

963
01:03:47,060 --> 01:03:48,460
Exactly.

964
01:03:48,460 --> 01:03:58,100
With that said... shall we move on to the next segment?

965
01:03:58,100 --> 01:04:00,180
The next segment.

966
01:04:00,180 --> 01:04:07,180
Before that, let me dismiss our listeners for a moment.

967
01:04:07,180 --> 01:04:08,100
That's all.

968
01:04:08,100 --> 01:04:09,100
Yes.

969
01:04:09,100 --> 01:04:09,980
That was our ordinary-letters segment.

970
01:04:09,980 --> 01:04:11,140
Thank you.

971
01:04:11,140 --> 01:04:12,300
Thank you very much.

972
01:04:12,300 --> 01:04:13,220
Okay.

973
01:04:13,220 --> 01:04:16,220
Oh, speaking of which...

974
01:04:16,220 --> 01:04:16,660
Ah!

975
01:04:16,660 --> 01:04:23,060
There will be fireworks nearby shortly.

976
01:04:23,060 --> 01:04:24,060
Really?

977
01:04:24,060 --> 01:04:26,860
I'd like to watch them together.

978
01:04:26,860 --> 01:04:31,100
There's a balcony with an amazing view.

979
01:04:31,100 --> 01:04:32,860
Wonderful!

980
01:04:32,860 --> 01:04:34,860
Shall we move over there?

981
01:04:34,860 --> 01:04:36,860
Let's go!

982
01:04:36,860 --> 01:04:37,860
Then...

983
01:04:37,860 --> 01:04:38,860
The fireworks upstairs.

984
01:04:38,860 --> 01:04:39,820
Please show me the way.

985
01:04:39,820 --> 01:04:41,660
All right, let's go.

986
01:04:41,660 --> 01:04:44,420
Lulu-chan knows the Demon King's castle remarkably well.

987
01:04:44,420 --> 01:04:45,540
There.

988
01:04:45,540 --> 01:04:46,660
Open it.

989
01:04:46,660 --> 01:04:48,820
Open it.

990
01:04:48,820 --> 01:04:49,940
Open it.

991
01:04:49,940 --> 01:04:50,940
Open it.

992
01:04:50,940 --> 01:04:52,940
Mito Radio!

993
01:04:52,940 --> 01:04:54,940
It's beautiful!

994
01:04:54,940 --> 01:04:56,940
We're here.

995
01:04:56,940 --> 01:04:58,940
Wow, this is gorgeous.

996
01:04:58,940 --> 01:05:00,940
It really is beautiful.

997
01:05:00,940 --> 01:05:02,940
Watching fireworks from here is incredible.

998
01:05:02,940 --> 01:05:04,940
The balcony.

999
01:05:04,940 --> 01:05:06,940
It feels like a place for a one-on-one duel.

1000
01:05:06,940 --> 01:05:07,940
I can see why this would be the setting.

1001
01:05:07,940 --> 01:05:08,940
It's truly beautiful.

1002
01:05:09,820 --> 01:05:17,020
It has the atmosphere of a duel between the hero and Demon King.

1003
01:05:17,020 --> 01:05:25,500
We could duel here—but never mind. Let's continue from this spot.

1004
01:05:25,500 --> 01:05:31,980
Our next segment is “The Returnee from the Demon Realm's Urashima Quiz.”

1005
01:05:31,980 --> 01:05:34,360
Oh!

1006
01:05:34,360 --> 01:05:45,860
We collected events from Nijisanji that happened while Suzuhara Lulu was away—events where it's almost impossible to tell truth from fiction.

1007
01:05:45,860 --> 01:05:58,480
Lulu-chan will deduce whether each one is true or false. If you already know one, simply say so.

1008
01:05:58,480 --> 01:06:04,340
I'm not confident. But this sounds fun! You're an Urashima Taro here, so nobody could blame you for not knowing.

1009
01:06:04,340 --> 01:06:08,020
A lot has happened, after all.

1010
01:06:08,020 --> 01:06:14,340
First, decide whether this story is true or false.

1011
01:06:14,340 --> 01:06:22,440
“A Nijisanji Liver accidentally said ‘my husband’ on stream and thereby revealed that she had a boyfriend.”

1012
01:06:22,440 --> 01:06:29,140
